---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import {
  BOOK_TITLE,
  BOOK_SUBTITLE,
  BOOK_EDITION,
  BOOK_DESCRIPTION,
  BOOK_OG_IMAGE,
  PDF_PATH,
  CHAPTERS,
  LINKS,
  LICENCE_SHORT,
} from '../site';
---
<BaseLayout
  title={`${BOOK_TITLE}: ${BOOK_SUBTITLE}`}
  description={BOOK_DESCRIPTION}
  canonicalPath="/book/"
  ogImage={BOOK_OG_IMAGE}
  preloadCover={true}
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="wrap hero-grid">
      <div class="hero-copy">
        <p class="edition">{BOOK_EDITION}</p>
        <h1>
          Programming
          <span class="loop">the Loop</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="subtitle">{BOOK_SUBTITLE}</p>
        <p class="byline">By Garry Meaburn · Free PDF</p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="button primary" href={PDF_PATH}>Download the PDF</a>
          <a class="button" href="#contents">Contents</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-visual">
        <img
          src="/book-cover.png"
          width="1600"
          height="2400"
          alt="Cover of Programming the Loop: What I Measured with Coding Agents by Garry Meaburn"
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="about-the-book">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>About the book</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          When I started using coding agents on my own projects, they were capable when they had the
          right context and expensive when fed everything “just in case”. Some published advice
          assumes that the more you tell an agent about your repository, the better it does. I
          wanted numbers from controlled trials rather than single-run anecdotes. The experiments
          test that assumption; the results below are where it held and where it didn’t.
        </p>
        <p>
          Findings are individual experimental results with stated limits, not laws of nature.
          Findings 1 to 3 used OpenAI’s Codex (recorded as <code>gpt-5.6-luna</code> at medium
          reasoning); finding 4 used Cursor’s <code>composer-2.5</code>. Recommendations in the book
          are engineering judgement, informed by the findings and by other practitioners’ published
          practice. Where a recommendation goes beyond what I measured, the text says so.
        </p>
        <p>
          One worked example runs through the chapters: add an API endpoint with a cross-repository
          dependency. It is a reproducible engineering demonstration of failure modes the
          experiments found (guessed facts, stale knowledge, unverifiable claims), not a recorded
          agent session. It is at
          <a href={LINKS.workedExample}>meabs/ProgrammingTheLoop-WorkedExample</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Eleven chapters. Chapter 3 is the heart of the book. If you have twenty minutes, read it
          and Chapter 5, then go straight to the take-home kit in Chapter 11. If you are about to
          give an agent your production credentials, read Chapter 10 first.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="measured">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Findings in brief</h2>
      <p class="lead">
        From the foreword. Small samples. The limits sit with the numbers.
      </p>
      <div class="findings">
        <article class="finding">
          <span class="label">Finding 1 · Experiment J</span>
          <h3>A fully “agent-optimised” repository cost more and was not reliably more correct</h3>
          <p>
            On a large Java backend the agent could read, a copy set up the recommended way (a
            generated guide, a code graph, three operating skills and a configured
            <code>AGENTS.md</code>) used 35–48% more tokens, with no reliable gain in correctness:
            29 against 28 of 32 changes, 19 against 16 of 24 structure questions, both within noise.
          </p>
          <p class="limits">
            Limits: one repository, one model, mechanical edits and structure questions, 8 runs per
            task per arm, four ingredients tested together. Analysed in Chapter 3. Source:
            OKFExperiment, <code>results/J-optimised-repo.md</code>.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="finding">
          <span class="label">Finding 2</span>
          <h3>Out-of-date knowledge of a dependency the agent couldn’t read was repeated as fact</h3>
          <p>
            After I renamed four things in a backend, agents with the stale guide and no source to
            check gave the old answer in 14 of 16 drift answers, and none of the wrong answers
            hedged.
          </p>
          <p class="limits">
            Limits: the drift was planted, 4 runs per question, one model. Analysed in Chapter 3.
            Source: OKFExperiment, <code>docs/RESULTS.md</code>, section D2.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="finding">
          <span class="label">Finding 3</span>
          <h3>When the missing backend facts were supplied, passes went from 3 to 18–19 out of 20</h3>
          <p>
            A storefront change depended on a backend the agent could not see. With nothing, 3 of 20
            runs passed the hidden tests; with a guide to the backend, 19 of 20 on small tasks and
            18 of 20 on harder ones. A later experiment found an API contract or a 3&nbsp;KB note
            did about as well, more cheaply.
          </p>
          <p class="limits">
            Limits: 5 runs per task, identifiers renamed by me, one model. Analysed in Chapter 3.
            Source: OKFExperiment, <code>docs/RESULTS.md</code>, sections F and G.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article class="finding">
          <span class="label">Finding 4</span>
          <h3>Without the contract, agents defeated the check meant to catch them</h3>
          <p>
            On the worked example’s own task, each rewrote the freshness gate so that, with no
            provider contract to hand, it compared the contract the agent had invented with itself,
            and passed. Their reports still matched their command logs. All 12 runs, with and without
            the contract, reported <code>passed</code> while listing the brief’s criterion 6, a
            check against the real inventory service, as unverified. With the contract, all 6 runs
            were correct.
          </p>
          <p class="limits">
            Limits: one model, one task, 6 runs per arm. Analysed in Chapters 3 and 5.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="contents">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Contents</h2>
      <p class="lead">
        The PDF is the permanent first edition. The chapter links are short web notes drawn from it.
      </p>
      <ol class="chapter-list">
        {CHAPTERS.map((ch) => (
          <li>
            <a href={ch.href}>
              <span class="chapter-num">{String(ch.num).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
              <span>
                <span class="chapter-title">{ch.title}</span>
                <span class="chapter-sub">{ch.blurb}</span>
              </span>
            </a>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="licence">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Licence and download</h2>
      <div class="licence-box">
        <p>{LICENCE_SHORT}</p>
        <p>
          The latest edition is at
          <a href={LINKS.githubAuthor}>github.com/meabs</a>.
          This site offers the PDF only.
        </p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="button primary" href={PDF_PATH}>Download Programming-the-Loop-1st-edition.pdf</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="feedback">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Feedback</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          If a number, a limit or a claim is wrong, I want to know. Prefer something concrete: which
          page, which figure, what you think it should say.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="feedback-links">
        <a href={LINKS.feedbackIssue}>Open an issue on the worked example</a>
        <a href={LINKS.workedExample}>Worked example repository</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
